<% content_for :title, "How your content reaches a model" %>

<%# The single learning resource behind /test. Written to be SKIMMED: a fact per line, a table or a
    diagram wherever one replaces a paragraph, and a source link on every claim. Prose is the
    fallback, not the default.

    Deep-linked from results, so ids are an API. These are referenced by name and must not be
    renamed: actions.rb (clean-text, code-channel, crawlers, licenses, outcomes, quality-filter,
    reachable, sitemap, software-heritage, web-channel), discoverability.rb (+ llms-txt),
    funnel.rb (+ copies, memorization, the-stack), license.rb (license-implications), and
    ApplicationHelper::LEARN_TERMS (the-stack, swh, common-crawl, fineweb-edu, ccbot).
    test/integration/analyses_flow_test.rb scans those files and asserts each id is present. %>
<article class="learn">
  <header class="learn__head">
    <p class="learn__kicker">Learn</p>
    <h1>How your content reaches a model</h1>
    <p class="learn__lead">
      Two systems decide whether an LLM knows your product. <strong>Training</strong> already
      happened and cannot be changed. <strong>Retrieval</strong> runs per question and you can
      change it today.
    </p>
    <p class="learn__meta">
      The model behind <a href="/test">the checks on /test</a>. Every number is measured and links
      to its working; every rule links to the document that states it.
    </p>
  </header>

  <nav class="learn__toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#start">Checklist</a></li>
      <li><a href="#two-systems">Two systems</a></li>
      <li><a href="#training">Training: collected, then filtered</a></li>
      <li><a href="#retrieval">Retrieval: what an agent fetches today</a></li>
      <li><a href="#agent-repo">Your repo: discovery, evaluation, licences</a></li>
      <li><a href="#outcomes">Where you stand</a></li>
      <li><a href="#tools">Check your own pages</a></li>
      <li><a href="#glossary">Glossary</a></li>
      <li><a href="#limits">Limits</a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <%# --- 0. checklist ------------------------------------------------------------------------ %>
  <section id="start">
    <h2>Checklist</h2>
    <p class="learn__meta">Ordered by measured impact.</p>

    <h3>Training</h3>
    <ol class="learn__do">
      <li>
        <strong>Put the docs source in a public GitHub repo.</strong>
        <span class="learn__tag learn__tag--high">highest value</span>
        <span class="learn__why">The only channel with no prose filter.
        <a href="#code-channel">How &rarr;</a></span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Use a permissive licence, or none.</strong>
        <span class="learn__why">The Stack keeps <code>permissive</code> and <code>no_license</code>;
        drops <code>non_permissive</code>. <a href="#licenses">Which &rarr;</a></span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Check the repo against the Am-I-in-The-Stack index, and confirm Software
        Heritage archived it.</strong>
        <span class="learn__why">Membership is observable; archival is the collection path you
        can trigger yourself. <a href="#software-heritage">Why &rarr;</a></span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Write the README and a runnable <code>examples/</code>.</strong>
        <span class="learn__why">Usually the most-trained text about a library.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Own the snippet people copy.</strong>
        <span class="learn__why">Copies predict recall at ρ = +0.70. Stars predict nothing.
        <a href="#copies">Data &rarr;</a></span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Open posts with what the reader will learn.</strong>
        <span class="learn__why">Only the first ~512 tokens are scored.
        <a href="#quality-filter">What scores &rarr;</a></span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Earn links to deep pages.</strong>
        <span class="learn__why">Common Crawl never copies a domain in full.
        <a href="#web-channel">How it samples &rarr;</a></span>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Retrieval</h3>
    <ol class="learn__do">
      <li>
        <strong>Verify a crawler actually gets a 200.</strong>
        <span class="learn__tag learn__tag--high">most often broken</span>
        <span class="learn__why"><code>robots.txt</code> and your WAF disagree.
        <a href="#reachable">Why &rarr;</a></span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Serve markdown: <code>Accept: text/markdown</code> or a <code>.md</code> twin.</strong>
        <span class="learn__why">Saves the agent parsing your theme.
        <a href="#clean-text">How &rarr;</a></span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ship <code>sitemap.xml</code>, <code>301</code> moved URLs, cut click depth.</strong>
        <span class="learn__why"><a href="#sitemap">Crawl budget &rarr;</a></span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Decide per crawler, not per company.</strong>
        <span class="learn__why">Nine user agents, three jobs, different costs.
        <a href="#crawlers">Table &rarr;</a></span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Measure.</strong>
        <span class="learn__why"><a href="/test">/test</a> for all checks,
        <a href="/check">/check</a> for the quality score alone.</span>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Skip</h3>
    <ul class="learn__skip">
      <li><strong>Blocking <code>CCBot</code>, <code>ClaudeBot</code>, <code>GPTBot</code></strong>
        if you want inclusion. The one lever proven to work, in the direction of exclusion.</li>
      <li><strong>Private repos and copyleft.</strong> Never collected, or dropped by name.</li>
      <li><strong>Chasing stars.</strong> Not a filter in any Stack version. Gates forks only, at 5+.</li>
      <li><strong>Mirroring your docs into other repos.</strong> MinHash collapses copies into one.</li>
      <li><strong>Stripping boilerplate.</strong> Measured: 2.43 &rarr; 2.48, and worse for reference
        pages. <a href="#quality-filter">Chart &rarr;</a></li>
      <li><strong>Opening with war stories, benchmarks or announcements.</strong> Genre caps the score.</li>
      <li><strong><code>llms.txt</code>.</strong>
        <a href="https://www.seroundtable.com/google-does-not-endorse-llms-txt-40789.html" rel="noopener">No
        major AI system uses it.</a> <a href="#llms-txt">More &rarr;</a></li>
      <li><strong>Relying on the rendered docs site alone.</strong> 0 of 118 pages cleared the filter.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <%# --- 1. two systems ---------------------------------------------------------------------- %>
  <section id="two-systems">
    <h2>Two systems</h2>

    <div class="learn__split">
      <div class="learn__col">
        <h3>Training</h3>
        <p class="learn__colmeta">Already decided</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Collected</strong> by a web crawler or from public code hosting.</li>
          <li>Then <strong>filtered</strong> for quality, licence and duplication.</li>
          <li>Nothing leaves a corpus that shipped.</li>
          <li>A version number learned two years ago stays learned.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="learn__col">
        <h3>Retrieval</h3>
        <p class="learn__colmeta">Fixable today</p>
        <ul>
          <li>An agent fetches a URL mid-answer, or reads a recent index.</li>
          <li><code>robots.txt</code>, your WAF and your markup decide what it gets.</li>
          <li>A deploy today applies to the next question.</li>
          <li>Only happens when something points the agent at you.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figure class="learn__fig">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 250" class="learn__svg" role="img" aria-labelledby="fig1-t fig1-d">
        <title id="fig1-t">The two gates into a training corpus</title>
        <desc id="fig1-d">Your docs site is collected by Common Crawl, then meets a quality filter
          that discards around 90 percent of what it sees. Your docs repo is archived by Software
          Heritage, then meets only a license and duplicate check, with no prose-quality filter.
          Both routes end in the training corpus.</desc>

