        <rect class="svg-box svg-box--warn" x="170" y="42" width="155" height="66" rx="8"/>
        <text class="svg-t" x="247" y="70" text-anchor="middle">Common Crawl
          <tspan class="svg-s" x="247" dy="17">samples by rank</tspan></text>
        <path class="svg-arrow" d="M329 75 H351"/>

        <rect class="svg-box svg-box--bad" x="355" y="42" width="185" height="66" rx="8"/>
        <text class="svg-t" x="447" y="65" text-anchor="middle">Quality classifier
          <tspan class="svg-s" x="447" dy="16">keeps score ≥ 2.5</tspan>
          <tspan class="svg-s" x="447" dy="15">~90% discarded</tspan></text>
        <path class="svg-arrow" d="M544 75 H566"/>

        <%# Code lane %>
        <rect class="svg-box" x="8" y="146" width="132" height="66" rx="8"/>
        <text class="svg-t" x="74" y="174" text-anchor="middle">Your docs repo
          <tspan class="svg-s" x="74" dy="17">Markdown in git</tspan></text>
        <path class="svg-arrow" d="M144 179 H166"/>

        <rect class="svg-box svg-box--warn" x="170" y="146" width="155" height="66" rx="8"/>
        <text class="svg-t" x="247" y="174" text-anchor="middle">Software Heritage
          <tspan class="svg-s" x="247" dy="17">archives the repo</tspan></text>
        <path class="svg-arrow" d="M329 179 H351"/>

        <rect class="svg-box svg-box--ok" x="355" y="146" width="185" height="66" rx="8"/>
        <text class="svg-t" x="447" y="169" text-anchor="middle">License and duplicates
          <tspan class="svg-s svg-s--strong" x="447" dy="16">no prose filter at all</tspan></text>
        <path class="svg-arrow" d="M544 179 H566"/>

        <%# Shared outcome %>
        <rect class="svg-box svg-box--ink" x="570" y="42" width="142" height="170" rx="8"/>
        <text class="svg-t svg-t--inv" x="641" y="120" text-anchor="middle">Training
          <tspan class="svg-t svg-t--inv" x="641" dy="18">corpus</tspan></text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        Both routes need collection. Only the web route is filtered for prose quality, which is why
        the same reference page can fail one and pass the other.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p class="learn__note">
      Training makes you the default answer when nobody looks anything up. Retrieval keeps that
      answer current.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%# --- 2. training ------------------------------------------------------------------------- %>
  <section id="training">
    <h2>Training: collected, then filtered</h2>

    <ul class="learn__facts">
      <li>Public and crawlable clears <strong>gate 1 only</strong>.</li>
      <li>Web pipelines discard around <strong>90%</strong> of what they collect
        (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.01116" rel="noopener">RefinedWeb</a>).</li>
      <li>Your four surfaces meet different gates.</li>
    </ul>

    <figure class="learn__fig">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 232" class="learn__svg" role="img" aria-labelledby="fig2-t fig2-d">
        <title id="fig2-t">The four channels and the gate each one meets</title>
        <desc id="fig2-d">Code and docs in repos are collected by GitHub crawls and Software
          Heritage and face no prose gate. Blog posts, rendered documentation and marketing pages are all collected by
          Common Crawl and face the FineWeb-Edu classifier, which rewards tutorial prose, is hostile
          to reference material and rewards marketing pages almost not at all.</desc>

        <text class="svg-cap" x="18"  y="16">SURFACE</text>
        <text class="svg-cap" x="220" y="16">COLLECTED BY</text>
        <text class="svg-cap" x="360" y="16">QUALITY GATE</text>
        <text class="svg-cap" x="515" y="16">WHAT WE MEASURED</text>

        <%# Row 1: repos %>
        <rect class="svg-lane svg-lane--ok" x="4" y="28" width="712" height="44" rx="8"/>
        <rect class="svg-pip svg-pip--ok" x="4" y="28" width="4" height="44"/>
        <text class="svg-t" x="18"  y="55">Code and docs in repos</text>
        <text class="svg-s" x="220" y="55">GitHub crawls + SWH</text>
        <text class="svg-t svg-t--ok" x="360" y="55">none</text>
        <text class="svg-s" x="515" y="55">license and dedup only</text>

        <%# Row 2: blog %>
        <rect class="svg-lane svg-lane--warn" x="4" y="78" width="712" height="44" rx="8"/>
        <rect class="svg-pip svg-pip--warn" x="4" y="78" width="4" height="44"/>
        <text class="svg-t" x="18"  y="105">Blog posts</text>
        <text class="svg-s" x="220" y="105">Common Crawl</text>
        <text class="svg-t svg-t--warn" x="360" y="105">FineWeb-Edu ≥ 2.5</text>
        <text class="svg-s" x="515" y="105">clears it once rewritten</text>

        <%# Row 3: rendered docs %>
        <rect class="svg-lane svg-lane--bad" x="4" y="128" width="712" height="44" rx="8"/>
        <rect class="svg-pip svg-pip--bad" x="4" y="128" width="4" height="44"/>
        <text class="svg-t" x="18"  y="155">Rendered docs</text>
        <text class="svg-s" x="220" y="155">Common Crawl</text>
        <text class="svg-t svg-t--bad" x="360" y="155">FineWeb-Edu ≥ 2.5</text>
        <text class="svg-s" x="515" y="155">0 of 118 pages cleared</text>

        <%# Row 4: marketing %>
        <rect class="svg-lane svg-lane--bad" x="4" y="178" width="712" height="44" rx="8"/>
        <rect class="svg-pip svg-pip--bad" x="4" y="178" width="4" height="44"/>
        <text class="svg-t" x="18"  y="205">Marketing pages</text>
        <text class="svg-s" x="220" y="205">Common Crawl</text>
        <text class="svg-t svg-t--bad" x="360" y="205">FineWeb-Edu ≥ 2.5</text>
        <text class="svg-s" x="515" y="205">rewards almost nothing</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        The measured column reports pages on docs.anycable.io scored with the open classifier.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3 id="web-channel">Common Crawl samples; it does not copy you</h3>
    <ul class="learn__facts">
      <li>Ranks domains by harmonic centrality, budgets crawling by rank, and
        <a href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3630106.3659033" rel="noopener">never copies a
        domain in full</a>.</li>
      <li>Misses new, deep, weakly linked pages.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="learn__tablewrap">
      <table class="learn__table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Site</th><th>In Common Crawl</th><th>Score, crawled</th><th>Score, missed</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>evilmartians.com</td><td>221 of 600 (37%)</td><td>1.52</td><td>1.51</td></tr>
          <tr><td>docs.anycable.io</td><td>34 of 125 (27%)</td><td>1.83</td><td>1.83</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="learn__note">
      Crawled and missed pages score the same. The gap is link sampling, not writing quality.
    </p>

    <h3 id="quality-filter">The filter rewards school-style explanation</h3>
    <ul class="learn__facts">
      <li><a href="#fineweb-edu">FineWeb-Edu</a> keeps a document scoring <strong>2.5 or higher</strong>
        (<a href="https://huggingface.co/HuggingFaceFW/fineweb-edu-classifier" rel="noopener">classifier</a>).</li>
      <li>Deliberately
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.17557" rel="noopener">tuned toward school-level
        explanation and away from technical pages</a>.</li>
      <li>Scores the <strong>first ~512 tokens only</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <figure class="learn__fig">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 268" class="learn__svg" role="img" aria-labelledby="fig3-t fig3-d">
        <title id="fig3-t">Quality scores before and after rewriting</title>
        <desc id="fig3-d">A blog post scores 2.43 as published, 2.48 with boilerplate stripped, 3.19
          with the lead rewritten to teach, and 3.54 rewritten as a textbook section. A reference
          documentation page scores 2.44, 2.11, 2.56 and 2.83 through the same four versions. The
          keep line is 2.5. Rewriting the lead takes the blog post over the line; the reference page
          only just crosses it when fully rewritten.</desc>

        <%# legend %>
        <rect class="svg-bar svg-bar--a" x="212" y="8" width="10" height="10" rx="2"/>
        <text class="svg-s" x="228" y="17">Blog post</text>
        <rect class="svg-bar svg-bar--b" x="300" y="8" width="10" height="10" rx="2"/>
        <text class="svg-s" x="316" y="17">Reference docs page</text>

        <%# axis: 0 to 4 maps x 206 to 692 (121.5 px per unit) %>
        <line class="svg-axis" x1="206" y1="30" x2="206" y2="248"/>
        <line class="svg-keep" x1="509.75" y1="30" x2="509.75" y2="248"/>
        <text class="svg-cap svg-cap--keep" x="509.75" y="262" text-anchor="middle">KEEP LINE 2.5</text>

        <%# group 1: as published %>
        <text class="svg-t" x="8" y="55">As published</text>
        <rect class="svg-bar svg-bar--a" x="206" y="42" width="295.2" height="16" rx="3"/>
        <text class="svg-v" x="516" y="55">2.43</text>
        <rect class="svg-bar svg-bar--b" x="206" y="62" width="296.5" height="16" rx="3"/>
        <text class="svg-v" x="516" y="75">2.44</text>

        <%# group 2: boilerplate stripped %>
        <text class="svg-t" x="8" y="110">Boilerplate stripped</text>
        <rect class="svg-bar svg-bar--a" x="206" y="97" width="301.3" height="16" rx="3"/>
        <text class="svg-v" x="515" y="110">2.48</text>
        <rect class="svg-bar svg-bar--b" x="206" y="117" width="256.4" height="16" rx="3"/>
        <text class="svg-v" x="470" y="130">2.11</text>

        <%# group 3: lead rewritten %>
        <text class="svg-t" x="8" y="165">Lead rewritten to teach</text>
        <rect class="svg-bar svg-bar--a" x="206" y="152" width="387.6" height="16" rx="3"/>
        <text class="svg-v" x="601" y="165">3.19</text>
        <rect class="svg-bar svg-bar--b" x="206" y="172" width="311" height="16" rx="3"/>
        <text class="svg-v" x="525" y="185">2.56</text>

        <%# group 4: textbook %>
        <text class="svg-t" x="8" y="220">Rewritten as a textbook</text>
        <rect class="svg-bar svg-bar--a" x="206" y="207" width="430.1" height="16" rx="3"/>
        <text class="svg-v" x="644" y="220">3.54</text>
        <rect class="svg-bar svg-bar--b" x="206" y="227" width="343.8" height="16" rx="3"/>
        <text class="svg-v" x="558" y="240">2.83</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        Rewriting the lead so it explains what the reader will learn is what moves a blog post over
        the line. Stripping boilerplate moves nothing and makes the reference page worse. Genre sets
        the ceiling.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="learn__tablewrap">
      <table class="learn__table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Site</th><th>Pages scored</th><th>Clearing 2.5</th><th>Best page</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>evilmartians.com</td><td>598</td><td>5</td><td>3.12</td></tr>
          <tr><td>docs.anycable.io</td><td>118</td><td>0</td><td>2.44</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3 id="code-channel">The code channel has no prose filter</h3>
    <ul class="learn__facts">
      <li>Licence check and duplicate check. Nothing else.</li>
      <li>A page scoring 1.6 on the web filter is collected without complaint from a public repo.</li>
      <li><a href="#the-stack">The Stack</a> v1 held about
        <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/bigcode/the-stack" rel="noopener">254 GB of Markdown</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <ol class="learn__steps">
      <li><strong>The repo is public.</strong> Private content never enters a code corpus.</li>
      <li><strong id="software-heritage">The corpus collected it.</strong>
        <a href="#the-stack">The Stack v2</a> was built from the
        <a href="https://archive.softwareheritage.org/" rel="noopener">Software Heritage</a> archive,
        where archival is neither automatic nor star-driven: <code>workos/authkit</code> has 3.3k
        stars, an MIT licence, and no record there. v3 crawls GitHub directly (GH Archive events
        plus the SWH graph, cutoff 2025-08-07), so a repo public after that date waits for the next
        snapshot no matter how it is licensed. Membership in v3 is directly checkable via the
        official <a href="https://huggingface.co/spaces/HuggingFaceCode/in-the-stack"
        rel="noopener">Am I in The Stack?</a> index, and opting out works: the <code>rspec</code>
        and <code>bridgetownrb</code> orgs asked out and are absent org-wide.</li>
      <li><strong id="licenses">The licence is not disqualifying.</strong> v2 and v3 keep
        <code>permissive</code> <em>and</em> <code>no_license</code>, and drop
        <code>non_permissive</code>: GPL and similar, all-rights-reserved, and source-available
        licences such as BUSL, FSL and SSPL
        (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.19173" rel="noopener">StarCoder2 / The Stack v2</a>).
        v1 dropped unlicensed files; that rule is three dataset generations old.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="learn__note">
      GitHub reports <code>NOASSERTION</code> for every licence it cannot match, so
      <code>spdx_id</code> cannot separate an Elastic-licensed repo from an unlicensed weekend
      project. Our check reads the LICENSE text, and treats the observed index as the last word:
      v3 filters file by file, and its scanner's calls surprise in both directions
      (<code>karafka/wiki</code> is in with an all-rights-reserved licence file;
      <code>karafka/karafka</code>, LGPL, is out; <code>sidekiq/sidekiq</code>, also LGPL, is in).
    </p>

    <details class="learn__ev">
      <summary>Measured: a missing licence suppresses nothing</summary>
      <div class="learn__tablewrap">
        <table class="learn__table">
          <thead><tr><th>Licence class</th><th>Repos with a verbatim hit</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>No licence</td><td>11 of 57 (19%)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Permissive</td><td>8 of 91 (9%)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Copyleft</td><td>0 of 9</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Source-available</td><td>0 of 2</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="learn__evnote">
        Fisher exact p = 0.079. Prominence is confounded with licence here, so the supportable
        reading is that no evidence shows a missing licence suppressing inclusion. The last two
        groups are too small to test.
      </p>
    </details>

    <h3 id="copies">Copies predict recall; stars do not</h3>
    <div class="learn__tablewrap">
      <table class="learn__table">
        <thead><tr><th>Predictor</th><th>Spearman ρ vs hit rate</th><th>p</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Independent copies <small>(15 repos, 38 passages)</small></td>
              <td><strong>+0.70</strong></td><td>0.005</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Same, different lab's model</td><td><strong>+0.68</strong></td><td>0.0065</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Stars <small>(168 repos, 309 passages)</small></td><td>+0.006</td><td>0.94</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Forks</td><td>+0.042</td><td>0.59</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Owner's top-repo stars</td><td>+0.012</td><td>0.88</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <ul class="learn__facts">
      <li><code>vercel/next.js</code> (141k stars) and <code>supabase/supabase</code> (107k):
        <strong>no verbatim runs</strong>.</li>
      <li><code>getsurfboard/manual</code> (115 stars): <strong>33-word</strong> exact run.</li>
      <li><code>tailwindlabs/tailwindcss.com</code>: no licence, ~140 stars, 68 independent copies,
        most memorized repo in that sample.</li>
      <li>MinHash-LSH removed about <strong>40%</strong> of permissively licensed files in The Stack
        v2, which is why mirroring your own docs adds nothing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="memorization">Verbatim recall proves inclusion; silence proves nothing</h3>
    <ul class="learn__facts">
      <li><strong>Method:</strong> give a model 55 words, measure the longest exact continuation,
        against a positive control (the MIT licence, must fire) and a fabricated passage (must not).</li>
      <li><strong>A long run is strong evidence.</strong> Text cannot be reproduced unless it was seen.</li>
      <li><strong>A null result is weak.</strong> Read it as "no proof found", never as "absent".</li>
      <li><strong>A short run is a hint.</strong> Nine words of "returns 204 No Content on success"
        is phrasing thousands of API docs share.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="learn__tablewrap">
      <table class="learn__table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Page</th><th>Quality score</th><th>Filter keeps it?</th><th>Verbatim recall</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Supabase Auth guide</td><td>1.43</td><td>no</td><td>yes, 23 words</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Resend Node.js quickstart</td><td>1.68</td><td>no</td><td>yes, 15 words</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="learn__note">
      Both failed the web filter and reached training through the code channel:
      <a href="https://github.com/supabase/supabase" rel="noopener">supabase/supabase</a> under
      Apache-2.0, and <a href="https://github.com/resend/resend-node" rel="noopener">resend-node</a>
      under MIT, copied into roughly 1,840 repositories. Supabase's Row Level Security guide sits in
      the same repo with no recall, because prose gets paraphrased instead of copied.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%# --- 3. retrieval ------------------------------------------------------------------------ %>
  <section id="retrieval">
    <h2>Retrieval: what an agent fetches today</h2>

    <h3 id="crawlers">One decision per crawler, not per company</h3>
    <div class="learn__tablewrap">
      <table class="learn__table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>User agent</th><th>Run by</th><th>Feeds</th><th>Blocking it costs you</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>CCBot</code></td><td>Common Crawl</td><td>Open web corpora</td>
              <td>The corpora most public datasets start from</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ClaudeBot</code></td><td>Anthropic</td><td>Training</td>
              <td>Collection for training</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>Claude-SearchBot</code></td><td>Anthropic</td><td>Search quality</td>
              <td>Visibility in Claude's search</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>Claude-User</code></td><td>Anthropic</td><td>User-triggered fetch</td>
              <td>Claude cannot open your page on request</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>GPTBot</code></td><td>OpenAI</td><td>Training</td>
              <td>Collection for training</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>OAI-SearchBot</code></td><td>OpenAI</td><td>ChatGPT search</td>
              <td>Visibility in ChatGPT search</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ChatGPT-User</code></td><td>OpenAI</td><td>User-triggered fetch</td>
              <td>OpenAI documents that robots.txt may not apply here</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>Google-Extended</code></td><td>Google</td><td>Gemini training, grounding</td>
              <td>Gemini only. Google documents Search as unaffected</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>Googlebot</code></td><td>Google</td><td>Search</td>
              <td>Google Search</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="learn__note">
      Published crawler lists:
      <a href="https://support.claude.com/en/articles/8896518-does-anthropic-crawl-data-from-the-web-and-how-can-site-owners-block-the-crawler" rel="noopener">Anthropic</a>,
      <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/bots" rel="noopener">OpenAI</a>,
      <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/google-common-crawlers" rel="noopener">Google</a>.
      All state their crawlers honour <code>robots.txt</code>. Our check reports the training
      crawlers, because that gate is the permanent one.
    </p>

    <h3 id="reachable">Allowed and reachable are different facts</h3>
    <ul class="learn__facts">
      <li><code>robots.txt</code> is a request. Your CDN, WAF or bot rule is the enforcement point.</li>
      <li>We fetch with a crawler user agent and report the status code that comes back.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="clean-text">Serve source text, not rendered HTML</h3>
    <ul class="learn__facts">
      <li><strong>Content negotiation:</strong> return <code>text/markdown</code> for
        <code>Accept: text/markdown</code>.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="#markdown-twin">Markdown twin</a>:</strong> the same page at a
        <code>.md</code> URL.</li>
      <li>Neither is required by any lab. Both decide whether an agent reads your sentences or
        parses your theme.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="sitemap">Crawl budget decides which pages exist to be found</h3>
    <ul class="learn__facts">
      <li>Without <code>sitemap.xml</code>, crawlers reach deep pages far less often.</li>
      <li>Redirect moved URLs with <code>301</code>, cut click depth, earn links to what matters.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="llms-txt">llms.txt is widely adopted and used by nobody</h3>
    <ul class="learn__facts">
      <li>A curated index of your docs for LLMs. Broad adoption, and
        <a href="https://www.seroundtable.com/google-does-not-endorse-llms-txt-40789.html" rel="noopener">no
        major AI system uses it</a> for training or inference.</li>
      <li>We report it as informational: present or absent, never as a win.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <%# --- 4. the repo an agent reads -----------------------------------------------------------
      Mechanisms rather than measurements, and labelled as such. The row ids are linked from
      Analyzer::Discoverability and Analyzer::License. %>
  <section id="agent-repo">
    <h2>Your repo: discovery, evaluation, licences</h2>

    <p id="discovery">
      An agent does three things with a library: <strong>discovers</strong> it,
      <strong>evaluates</strong> whether to trust it, <strong>suggests</strong> it. Install,
      configure, deploy and pay are separate problems that /test does not measure.
    </p>

    <p class="learn__note">
      These are mechanisms, not measurements. The crawler and corpus numbers above are measured; the
      repo signals below are how GitHub's own search and licence tooling work.
    </p>

    <div class="learn__tablewrap">
      <table class="learn__table">
        <thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Why it decides the answer</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" id="repo-readme">README</th>
            <td>An agent reproduces your description from it: the text GitHub serves on the repo
              page, and the file shipped in every package. Badges, a logo and a table of contents
              give it nothing to quote.</td>
            <td>Open with what the project does and who it is for, in sentences.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" id="repo-metadata">Description and topics</th>
            <td>The fields
              <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/search-github/searching-on-github/searching-for-repositories" rel="noopener">GitHub
              repository search matches</a>, alongside the name. Empty description means you are
              findable only by people who already know your name.</td>
            <td>One sentence, plus topics.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" id="repo-releases">Releases</th>
            <td>Without tags, the honest answer to "which version" is whatever is on
              <code>main</code> today. Releases drive the versions registries and changelogs
              expose.</td>
            <td>Tag releases.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" id="repo-activity">Recency</th>
            <td>Last-push dates are public and get read as a maintenance signal. Finished is a
              legitimate state; a silent repo does not say so.</td>
            <td>Say "stable, complete" in the README.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3 id="license-implications">A licence answers two different questions</h3>
    <ul class="learn__facts">
      <li>Whether a corpus keeps your files, and whether anyone may use them. The two answers
        routinely disagree.</li>
      <li>The case people get backwards: no <code>LICENSE</code> file. The Stack v2 and v3
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.19173" rel="noopener">keep unlicensed code</a>, while
        <a href="https://choosealicense.com/no-permission/" rel="noopener">default copyright</a>
        grants viewing and forking on GitHub and nothing more.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="learn__tablewrap">
      <table class="learn__table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Licence</th><th>An agent may suggest and paste it</th><th>Corpora keep the files</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD, ISC</th>
              <td>Yes, including commercially.</td><td>Yes.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">No <code>LICENSE</code> file</th>
              <td>No. Default copyright grants nobody permission.</td>
              <td>Yes, in v2 and v3.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">GPL, LGPL, AGPL, MPL</th>
              <td>With an obligation that follows the code. Many company policies block AGPL.</td>
              <td>No, dropped as non-permissive.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">BUSL, SSPL, Elastic, FSL, Commons Clause, PolyForm</th>
              <td>Restricted, usually against competing services.</td>
              <td>No, and GitHub reports every one as <code>NOASSERTION</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">CC-BY, CC-BY-SA</th>
              <td>Fine for prose. Share-alike follows anything reusing the text.</td>
              <td>Case by case.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="learn__note">
      <%= Analyzer::License::NOT_ADVICE %> The classification on /test reads the actual
      <code>LICENSE</code> text rather than GitHub's <code>spdx_id</code>, because every
      source-available licence reports as <code>NOASSERTION</code> in that field.
    </p>
  </section>

